// The Modern World, part 4: work, money, digital life and changing societies — the cost of living, start-ups, automation, online learning, cyberbullying, the digital divide, smart homes, housing, loneliness and urban farming.
ADDART({id:"costliving",t:"The Cost of Living",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🧾",w:"Cost of living",r:["inflation","money","labor","foodsecurity","housing"],
p:[["Everyday prices","The cost of living is how much money people need for food, housing, transport and other basics."],["When prices jump","After 2021, prices rose quickly in many countries, causing a cost-of-living crisis."],["Making ends meet","Families, businesses and governments respond in different ways."]],
l:"The cost of living is the amount of money needed to pay for basic needs such as food, housing, transport, electricity, water, health care and education in a particular place and time. When prices rise faster than incomes, people can afford less, and families struggle to make ends meet. From 2021 to 2023, many countries suffered a cost-of-living crisis, as prices for food, fuel and housing surged after the COVID-19 pandemic and Russia’s invasion of Ukraine. Governments and central banks responded with interest rate rises, subsidies, wage increases and price controls, which remain subjects of debate.",
s:[["Measuring prices","Governments track the cost of living using a consumer price index, which measures the prices of a basket of common goods and services over time. The rate at which these prices rise is called inflation. In the Philippines, the Philippine Statistics Authority publishes inflation figures every month."],["The crisis of the early 2020s","As economies reopened after pandemic lockdowns, demand recovered while supply chains were disrupted. Russia’s invasion of Ukraine in 2022 pushed up prices of oil, gas, wheat and fertilizer. Global inflation reached its highest levels in decades, and many households saw their real incomes fall."],["Who is hit hardest","Rising prices hurt poorer households most, because they spend a larger share of their income on food, fuel and rent. People on fixed incomes, such as pensioners, also suffer when prices rise faster than their payments."],["Wages and prices","When prices rise, workers often ask for higher wages. In the Philippines, regional wage boards set minimum wages. Economists debate how to raise incomes without pushing prices even higher."]],
k:[["Measure of prices","Consumer price index"],["Rise in prices","Inflation"],["Recent crisis","2021–2023"],["Philippine inflation data","Philippine Statistics Authority"],["Main tool of central banks","Interest rates"]],
d:[["1970s","Oil price shocks cause high inflation worldwide"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic disrupts supply chains"],["2022","Russia’s invasion of Ukraine pushes up energy and food prices"],["2022","Onion prices soar in the Philippines"],["2023","Philippine inflation peaks at its highest level in over a decade"],["2023","The Philippines sets a temporary ceiling on rice prices"]],
s2:[["The onion crisis","In late 2022 and early 2023, onion prices in the Philippines soared because of poor harvests, limited imports and hoarding, at one point costing more per kilo than meat. Some restaurants removed onions from their menus, and the episode became a symbol of rising food prices. Authorities investigated price manipulation and allowed more imports."],["Rice prices","Rice is the staple food of most Filipinos, so its price strongly affects household budgets. When rice prices rose in 2023, the government briefly set price ceilings, and in 2024 it cut import tariffs on rice to lower prices. Such measures help consumers but can affect farmers’ incomes, a long-running debate."],["Central banks","To slow inflation, central banks, such as the Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas, raise interest rates, making borrowing more expensive and cooling spending. Higher rates can bring inflation down but may also slow economic growth and raise loan costs."],["Shrinkflation","When costs rise, some companies keep prices the same but put less product in each package, a practice nicknamed shrinkflation. Shoppers may notice smaller chocolate bars or snack packs, and some governments require clearer labeling."],["Coping with high prices","Families cope by budgeting, buying in bulk, comparing prices, cooking at home, growing vegetables and cutting unnecessary spending. Community programs, cash transfers and food assistance help the most vulnerable."],["Debates","Economists and politicians disagree about how much governments should intervene with subsidies, price controls or tax cuts, which can provide quick relief but may be costly or distort markets, versus letting central banks and markets adjust. Views often depend on how people weigh short-term relief against long-term effects."]],
sp:["The basket of goods","To measure inflation, statisticians imagine a basket filled with the things a typical household buys: rice, bread, fish, meat, vegetables, cooking oil, rent, electricity, transport fares, school supplies, haircuts and more. Every month, they check the prices of these items in markets and stores across the country. If the basket costs more than it did a year earlier, prices have risen. The contents are updated over time; mobile phone plans and internet subscriptions were added as they became part of everyday life, while items such as typewriters were removed."],
g:[["Cost of living","The money needed to pay for basic needs"],["Inflation","A general rise in prices over time"],["Consumer price index","A measure of the prices of a basket of goods and services"],["Shrinkflation","Reducing product size while keeping the price"]],
f:["Prices surged worldwide from 2021 to 2023.","Inflation is measured with a consumer price index.","Onions briefly cost more than meat in the Philippines."],
fun:"In early 2023, onions in the Philippines briefly cost more per kilo than meat.",
tl:"Ang cost of living ay ang halagang kailangan para sa pagkain, bahay, pamasahe at iba pang pangangailangan. Mula 2021 hanggang 2023, mabilis tumaas ang presyo sa maraming bansa, kabilang ang Pilipinas, gaya ng sibuyas at bigas."});

ADDART({id:"startups",t:"Start-ups and Entrepreneurs",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🚀",w:"Startup company",r:["money","stockm","internet","gigeconomy","digitalpay"],
p:[["New businesses","A start-up is a young company trying to grow quickly with a new idea."],["Taking risks","Entrepreneurs start businesses, taking risks in hope of success."],["Billion-dollar start-ups","Start-ups valued at a billion dollars are called unicorns."]],
l:"A start-up is a newly founded company that aims to grow rapidly by offering a new product, service or way of doing business, often using technology. Start-ups are founded by entrepreneurs, people willing to take risks to turn ideas into businesses. Famous companies such as Apple, Google, Amazon, Alibaba and Grab began as start-ups, some in garages or small apartments. Many start-ups are funded by investors called venture capitalists, and those valued at over a billion dollars are nicknamed unicorns. Most start-ups fail, but successful ones can create jobs, new industries and great wealth.",
s:[["What makes a start-up","Unlike a typical small business, such as a neighborhood store, a start-up aims to scale quickly, reaching many customers, often across countries. Start-ups usually begin with a small team, an idea and little money, and they experiment rapidly to find what customers want."],["Funding","Founders often start with their own savings, money from family and friends or small grants. Angel investors and venture capital firms then invest in exchange for a share of ownership, hoping a few big successes will make up for many failures. Some start-ups eventually sell shares on the stock market."],["Garage legends","Many famous companies began humbly. Hewlett-Packard started in a garage in California in 1939, Apple in a garage in 1976 and Amazon in a garage in 1994. Silicon Valley in California became the world’s best-known start-up hub."],["Unicorns","In 2013 the investor Aileen Lee coined the term unicorn for privately held start-ups valued at over a billion US dollars, because such companies were then rare. Today there are hundreds of unicorns around the world."]],
k:[["Meaning","A young company aiming to grow quickly"],["Founders","Entrepreneurs"],["Main investors","Venture capitalists"],["Unicorn","A start-up valued at over $1 billion"],["Philippine law","Innovative Startup Act (2019)"]],
d:[["1939","Hewlett-Packard is founded in a garage"],["1976","Apple is founded"],["1999","Alibaba is founded in China"],["2012","Grab is founded"],["2013","The term unicorn is coined for billion-dollar start-ups"],["2019","The Philippines passes the Innovative Startup Act"]],
s2:[["Start-ups in Southeast Asia","Southeast Asia has produced major tech companies, such as Grab, which began in Malaysia and is now based in Singapore, Gojek and Tokopedia in Indonesia and Sea Group, owner of Shopee. They grew by solving local problems, such as hailing safe rides, delivering food and making payments for people without bank cards."],["Start-ups in the Philippines","The Philippine start-up scene has grown, especially in digital payments, online lending, logistics and education. The company behind GCash became the country’s first unicorn in 2021. The Innovative Startup Act of 2019 provides support such as grants, training and easier business registration."],["Africa and Latin America","Start-ups in Africa, such as the payments companies Flutterwave and M-Pesa’s successors, and in Latin America, such as MercadoLibre and Nubank, have grown rapidly by serving large populations with mobile technology."],["Failure","Most start-ups fail, often because there is not enough demand for their product, they run out of money or face strong competition. Many entrepreneurs see failure as a learning experience, and some successful founders failed several times before succeeding."],["Social enterprises","Some entrepreneurs start businesses mainly to solve social or environmental problems, such as providing clean water, affordable lighting or jobs for marginalized people, while still earning money. These are called social enterprises."],["Criticism","Critics say start-up culture can glorify overwork, encourage risky growth at any cost and concentrate wealth among a few founders and investors. Some high-profile start-ups collapsed after misleading investors. Supporters point to the innovations, jobs and services that start-ups create."]],
sp:["The garage in Palo Alto","In 1939 two young Stanford University graduates, Bill Hewlett and David Packard, set up a business in a small rented garage behind a house at 367 Addison Avenue in Palo Alto, California, with about 538 dollars. Their first major product was an audio oscillator, an electronic instrument for testing sound equipment; one of their first customers was Walt Disney Studios, which used it for the film Fantasia. Hewlett-Packard grew into a giant electronics company, and the garage is now a historic landmark known as the birthplace of Silicon Valley."],
g:[["Start-up","A young company aiming for rapid growth"],["Entrepreneur","A person who starts a business, taking risks"],["Venture capital","Money invested in young companies in exchange for ownership"],["Unicorn","A start-up valued at over a billion dollars"]],
f:["Many famous companies began as start-ups in garages.","A unicorn is a start-up worth over $1 billion.","The Philippines passed an Innovative Startup Act in 2019."],
fun:"One of Hewlett-Packard’s first customers was Walt Disney Studios.",
tl:"Ang start-up ay bagong kompanyang naglalayong lumago nang mabilis gamit ang bagong ideya. Ang mga negosyanteng nagsisimula nito ay tinatawag na entrepreneur. Ang start-up na nagkakahalaga ng mahigit isang bilyong dolyar ay tinatawag na unicorn."});

ADDART({id:"automation",t:"Automation and the Future of Work",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🤖",w:"Technological unemployment",r:["robot","ai","labor","gigeconomy","education"],
p:[["Machines at work","Automation means machines and software doing tasks once done by people."],["Jobs lost and created","Technology removes some jobs but creates new ones."],["Learning for the future","New skills help workers adapt as work changes."]],
l:"Automation is the use of machines, robots, computers and artificial intelligence to perform tasks that were once done by people. Since the Industrial Revolution, automation has transformed farming, factories and offices, eliminating some jobs while creating new ones and raising productivity. Today, advances in robotics and AI are spreading automation to new kinds of work, from driving and customer service to writing and analysis. People debate how many jobs may be affected, who will benefit and how societies should help workers adapt, through education, training and social protections.",
s:[["A long history","Machines have replaced human work for centuries, from water mills and steam engines to tractors, assembly lines and computers. In the early 1800s, English textile workers called Luddites smashed machines they feared would destroy their livelihoods. Over time, new industries created new jobs, though the transition was painful for many."],["Robots in factories","The first industrial robot, Unimate, began working on a General Motors assembly line in the United States in 1961, handling hot metal parts. Today millions of industrial robots weld, paint, assemble and pack goods. South Korea, Singapore, China, Germany and Japan use the most robots per worker."],["AI and office work","Artificial intelligence can now handle tasks such as answering customer questions, translating, summarizing documents, writing code and analyzing data. This means automation increasingly affects office and professional jobs, not just manual ones."],["Jobs that change","Most jobs are not entirely replaced; instead, some tasks are automated while others remain, changing what workers do. Tasks needing creativity, complex judgment, empathy, care and physical dexterity in unpredictable settings are harder to automate."]],
k:[["First industrial robot","Unimate (1961)"],["Early protesters","The Luddites (1811–1816)"],["Most robots per worker","South Korea"],["Jobs exposed to AI","Many, according to IMF estimates"],["Key response","Education and retraining"]],
d:[["1811","Luddites begin smashing textile machines in England"],["1913","Henry Ford introduces the moving assembly line"],["1961","The first industrial robot starts work"],["1997","A computer defeats the world chess champion"],["2013","A widely cited study estimates many jobs could be automated"],["2022","AI chatbots bring automation to writing and office tasks"]],
s2:[["How many jobs?","Estimates vary widely. A famous 2013 Oxford study estimated that about 47 percent of US jobs were at high risk of automation, while later studies gave lower figures. In 2024 the International Monetary Fund estimated that around 40 percent of jobs worldwide are exposed to AI, with some likely to benefit and others at risk. Many economists expect new jobs to emerge, as in past technological changes."],["The Philippines","The Philippines’ large business process outsourcing industry, including call centers and back-office work, employs well over a million people. AI tools that handle customer service and data processing could change many of these jobs, while also creating new roles in managing, training and checking AI systems. Training workers in new skills is a priority."],["New jobs","Technology has created jobs that did not exist a generation ago, such as app developers, data scientists, social media managers, drone pilots, cybersecurity experts and electric vehicle technicians. Care work, such as nursing and elderly care, is also growing."],["Responses","Ideas for helping workers include lifelong learning and retraining, stronger social safety nets, shorter working weeks and, more controversially, a universal basic income, regular payments to all citizens regardless of work. Countries are experimenting with different approaches."],["Who benefits?","Automation can raise productivity and lower prices, but its benefits may go mainly to company owners and highly skilled workers unless policies spread them more widely. How to share the gains fairly is a central question."],["Humans and machines together","Many experts emphasize working alongside machines, using technology to take over dangerous, dull or repetitive tasks so that people can focus on creative, social and complex work."]],
sp:["The Luddites","Between 1811 and 1816, skilled textile workers in the north of England, facing falling wages and unemployment, broke into workshops at night and smashed new mechanized looms and knitting frames. They claimed to follow a mysterious leader called General Ned Ludd, probably a legendary figure. The government sent thousands of soldiers to stop them and made machine-breaking punishable by death. Today the word Luddite is used for anyone who opposes new technology, but historians point out that the original Luddites were not against machines as such; they were protesting how machines were used to cut wages and worsen their working conditions."],
g:[["Automation","Using machines to do work once done by people"],["Industrial robot","A programmable machine used in manufacturing"],["Retraining","Learning new skills for a different job"],["Universal basic income","Regular payments to all citizens regardless of work"]],
f:["The first industrial robot began work in 1961.","The Luddites protested against machines in 1811–1816.","South Korea uses the most robots per worker."],
fun:"The original Luddites claimed to follow a leader called General Ned Ludd, who probably never existed.",
tl:"Ang automation ay paggamit ng makina, robot at artificial intelligence sa mga gawaing dating ginagawa ng tao. Nawawala ang ilang trabaho ngunit may mga bagong nalilikha. Mahalaga ang pag-aaral ng bagong kasanayan."});

ADDART({id:"onlinelearning",t:"Online Learning",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🎓",w:"Online learning",r:["education","internet","digitaldivide","covid19","remotework"],
p:[["Learning online","Online learning uses the internet to teach and learn."],["Classes for everyone","Free online courses let anyone study subjects from top universities."],["A pandemic test","During COVID-19, schools around the world moved to distance learning."]],
l:"Online learning, also called e-learning or distance learning when done remotely, is education delivered through the internet using videos, live classes, interactive exercises and digital materials. It ranges from online courses at universities and free lessons on websites to language-learning apps and video tutorials. Online learning makes education more flexible and accessible, but it depends on devices, internet access and self-discipline. During the COVID-19 pandemic, about 1.6 billion students were affected by school closures, and many schools, including in the Philippines, turned to online and modular distance learning, revealing both its promise and its limits.",
s:[["Learning at a distance","Distance learning is older than the internet. In the 1840s, Isaac Pitman taught shorthand by post. Radio and television later carried lessons, and the United Kingdom’s Open University, founded in 1969, taught students at home through broadcasts and printed materials."],["The internet era","From the 1990s, universities began offering online courses. In 2008 Khan Academy began posting free video lessons, and in 2012, called the year of the MOOC, massive open online courses from platforms such as Coursera and edX let millions study university subjects for free."],["Kinds of online learning","Some online classes are live, with teachers and students meeting by video. Others are self-paced, with recorded lessons and exercises. Blended learning combines online and face-to-face teaching. Apps teach languages, mathematics and coding through short, game-like lessons."],["Benefits","Online learning lets people study anytime and anywhere, at their own pace, often for free or at low cost. It can reach learners in remote areas, working adults and people with disabilities, and gives access to courses not available locally."]],
k:[["Also called","E-learning, distance learning"],["Open University founded","1969"],["Year of the MOOC","2012"],["Students affected by pandemic closures","About 1.6 billion"],["Philippine pandemic approach","Modular distance learning"]],
d:[["1840","Isaac Pitman teaches shorthand by post"],["1969","The Open University is founded in the United Kingdom"],["2008","Khan Academy begins posting free lessons"],["2012","Massive open online courses become popular"],["2020","School closures affect about 1.6 billion students worldwide"],["2022","Philippine schools return to full in-person classes"]],
s2:[["The Philippines during the pandemic","When schools closed in 2020, the Department of Education adopted distance learning. Most students used printed self-learning modules, while others had online classes or lessons on television and radio. Parents and older siblings often helped as home teachers. Schools returned to full in-person classes in November 2022."],["Challenges","Online learning requires a device, a reliable internet connection and a quiet place to study, which many students lack. It can be harder to stay motivated without classmates and teachers nearby, and young children especially need in-person interaction. Studies found significant learning losses during long school closures worldwide."],["Teachers online","Teachers had to learn new skills quickly during the pandemic, creating digital lessons, using video platforms and finding ways to keep students engaged. Many continue to use online tools in their classrooms."],["Learning anytime","Millions of people learn new skills online through video platforms, podcasts and courses, from cooking and music to programming and languages. Many Filipino workers take online courses to improve their job prospects."],["AI and learning","Artificial intelligence tools can now explain concepts, give feedback and act as tutors. Supporters see them as a way to give every student personal help; critics worry about cheating, errors and students relying on AI instead of thinking for themselves. Schools are developing rules for their use."],["The future","Most experts expect a mix of in-person and online learning. The pandemic showed that schools are important not just for lessons but for friendship, health, safety and support, while online tools can extend learning beyond the classroom."]],
sp:["Khan Academy began with a cousin","In 2004, Salman Khan, a financial analyst in the United States, began tutoring his young cousin Nadia in mathematics over the phone and internet. Soon other relatives wanted help too, so he started recording short video lessons and posting them on YouTube, where anyone could watch. His cousins said they preferred him on video, because they could pause and replay him without feeling embarrassed. The videos became so popular that in 2008 he founded Khan Academy, a nonprofit offering free lessons. Today it has lessons in dozens of languages used by millions of students around the world."],
g:[["Online learning","Education delivered through the internet"],["MOOC","Massive open online course"],["Blended learning","A mix of online and in-person teaching"],["Module","A self-learning booklet or unit of study"]],
f:["The Open University was founded in 1969.","2012 was called the year of the MOOC.","About 1.6 billion students were affected by pandemic school closures."],
fun:"Khan Academy began when Salman Khan tutored his cousin online.",
tl:"Ang online learning ay pag-aaral gamit ang internet. Noong pandemya ng COVID-19, gumamit ang mga paaralan sa Pilipinas ng modular at online distance learning. Kapaki-pakinabang ito ngunit nangangailangan ng gadget at internet."});

ADDART({id:"cyberbullying",t:"Cyberbullying",c:"Technology",i:"🛑",w:"Cyberbullying",r:["socialmedia","mentalyouth","dataprivacy","cybersecurity","misinformation"],
p:[["Bullying online","Cyberbullying is using phones, games or the internet to hurt, threaten or embarrass someone."],["It can be stopped","Blocking, reporting and telling a trusted adult help stop it."],["Be an upstander","Friends and bystanders can make a big difference."]],
l:"Cyberbullying is bullying that takes place through digital devices, such as phones, computers and game consoles, on social media, messaging apps, online games and other platforms. It includes sending hurtful messages, spreading rumors, sharing embarrassing photos or videos, excluding people from groups, impersonating them or threatening them. Because online content can spread quickly, reach many people and be hard to remove, cyberbullying can deeply hurt those targeted. Many countries, including the Philippines, have laws and school policies against it, and experts encourage young people to block, report and tell a trusted adult.",
s:[["Forms of cyberbullying","Cyberbullying can include name-calling and insults, spreading lies, posting embarrassing images, creating fake accounts to mock someone, deliberately leaving someone out of group chats, threatening messages and sharing private information or images without permission."],["How it differs","Unlike face-to-face bullying, cyberbullying can happen at any time of day, follow people home and reach large audiences. Bullies may hide behind anonymous accounts, and hurtful posts can be copied and shared widely."],["Effects","People who are cyberbullied may feel sad, anxious, embarrassed, angry or alone, and may avoid school or lose interest in activities. Cyberbullying can affect sleep, grades and mental health. Support from family, friends and schools helps people recover."],["How common is it","Surveys around the world suggest that a significant share of young people have experienced cyberbullying. A 2019 UNICEF poll of young people in 30 countries found that about one in three said they had been bullied online."]],
k:[["Where it happens","Social media, messaging apps, games"],["UNICEF 2019 poll","About 1 in 3 young people bullied online"],["Philippine law","Anti-Bullying Act of 2013"],["First steps","Don’t reply, save evidence, block, report, tell an adult"],["Stop Cyberbullying Day","A Friday in June"]],
d:[["1990s","Online chat rooms bring new forms of harassment"],["2000s","Social media and mobile phones spread cyberbullying"],["2013","The Philippines passes the Anti-Bullying Act"],["2019","The Philippines passes the Safe Spaces Act, covering online harassment"],["2019","A UNICEF poll highlights online bullying of young people"],["2020s","Platforms add more tools to block and report bullying"]],
s2:[["What to do if you are cyberbullied","Experts advise: do not reply or retaliate; take screenshots to save evidence; block the person; report the content to the platform; and tell a trusted adult, such as a parent, teacher or school counselor. If there are threats of violence, the police can help."],["Laws in the Philippines","The Anti-Bullying Act of 2013 requires schools to adopt policies against bullying, including cyberbullying. The Safe Spaces Act of 2019 covers gender-based harassment online, and other laws address threats, identity theft and sharing intimate images without consent."],["Upstanders","Bystanders who see cyberbullying can help by not sharing or liking hurtful content, supporting the person targeted with a kind message, reporting the content and telling an adult. Being an upstander rather than a bystander can stop bullying from spreading."],["Think before you post","Before posting or sharing, it helps to ask: Is it true? Is it kind? Would I say it face to face? Would I want it said about me? Online posts can last a long time and be seen by many people."],["Platforms and schools","Social media companies provide tools to block, mute, restrict and report users and to filter offensive comments. Schools teach digital citizenship, helping students understand respectful behavior online and how to get help."],["Kindness online","Campaigns encourage people to use the internet to spread kindness, such as posting encouraging messages, standing up for others and calling out hate speech. A positive online community makes the internet safer for everyone."]],
sp:["Pink shirts against bullying","In 2007, in Nova Scotia, Canada, a ninth-grade boy was bullied for wearing a pink shirt to school. Two older students, David Shepherd and Travis Price, decided to take a stand. They bought dozens of pink shirts and handed them out, encouraging classmates to wear them. The next day, hundreds of students came to school dressed in pink, and the bullies backed down. Their simple act grew into Pink Shirt Day, an anti-bullying day now marked in many countries, showing how bystanders acting together can stop bullying."],
g:[["Cyberbullying","Bullying through digital devices and the internet"],["Bystander","A person who sees something happen"],["Upstander","A person who acts to help when they see wrongdoing"],["Digital citizenship","Responsible and respectful behavior online"]],
f:["About one in three young people in a UNICEF poll said they were bullied online.","The Philippines passed the Anti-Bullying Act in 2013.","Save evidence, block, report and tell a trusted adult."],
fun:"Pink Shirt Day began when two students handed out pink shirts to stand up to bullies.",
tl:"Ang cyberbullying ay pananakit, pananakot o pagpapahiya sa isang tao gamit ang cellphone, laro o internet. Huwag gumanti, mag-screenshot ng ebidensiya, mag-block, mag-report at magsabi sa mapagkakatiwalaang matanda."});

ADDART({id:"digitaldivide",t:"The Digital Divide",c:"Technology",i:"📶",w:"Digital divide",r:["internet","inequality","onlinelearning","smartphone","sat"],
p:[["Connected and unconnected","The digital divide is the gap between people who have internet access and those who do not."],["Billions still offline","More than 2 billion people have never used the internet or rarely do."],["Closing the gap","Cheaper devices, better networks and digital skills help."]],
l:"The digital divide is the gap between people who have access to computers, smartphones and the internet, and the skills to use them, and those who do not. Although most of the world’s people are now online, more than 2 billion remain offline, most of them in low-income countries, rural areas and among women, older people and the poor. Lack of access can limit education, jobs, health care, banking and participation in society. Governments, companies and communities are working to close the gap through better networks, affordable devices and data, public Wi-Fi and digital skills training.",
s:[["Access","The first part of the divide is access: whether there are mobile networks, fiber cables or satellites to connect people, and whether they have devices. Remote islands, mountains and rural areas are often the hardest and most expensive to connect."],["Affordability","Even where networks exist, many people cannot afford smartphones, computers or data plans. The United Nations has set a goal that basic internet should cost no more than about 2 percent of average monthly income."],["Skills and use","Having access is not enough; people also need digital skills to use the internet safely and productively, to find reliable information, protect their privacy and avoid scams. Older people and those with less education often have fewer digital skills."],["Gender and other gaps","In many low-income countries, women are less likely than men to own a mobile phone or use the internet. People with disabilities may face barriers if websites and apps are not designed to be accessible."]],
k:[["People offline","More than 2 billion"],["Most affected","Low-income countries, rural areas, women, older people"],["UN affordability target","Basic internet under about 2% of monthly income"],["Philippine free Wi-Fi law","2017"],["Key solutions","Networks, affordable devices, digital skills"]],
d:[["1990s","The term digital divide comes into use"],["2005","The One Laptop per Child project is launched"],["2016","The UN declares internet access important for human rights"],["2017","The Philippines passes a law for free Wi-Fi in public places"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic highlights the digital divide"],["2020s","Satellite internet reaches more remote areas"]],
s2:[["The Philippines","Internet use in the Philippines has grown rapidly, and Filipinos are among the world’s heaviest social media users. But many remote barangays, islands and mountain communities have weak or no signal, and data costs are a burden for poor families. The Free Internet Access in Public Places Act of 2017 aims to provide free Wi-Fi in public places such as schools, plazas and hospitals, and new cell towers and satellite services are expanding coverage."],["Learning during the pandemic","When schools closed during the COVID-19 pandemic, students without devices or internet fell behind. In the Philippines, many families shared one phone among several children, and students climbed hills or trees to find a signal, which made the digital divide visible to everyone."],["Mobile phones first","In much of Africa and Asia, many people first connected to the internet through mobile phones rather than computers. Mobile money, such as M-Pesa in Kenya, showed how phones can bring services to people without bank branches or computers."],["Satellites and new networks","Satellite internet services can reach remote islands and villages where cables are too expensive. Other approaches include community networks built by local residents and undersea cables linking islands and countries."],["Libraries and community centers","Public libraries, schools and community centers provide computers, internet access and training for people who lack them at home. In the Philippines, local governments and nonprofit groups run digital literacy programs."],["Why it matters","As more services move online, from government forms and job applications to banking, health care and learning, people without access risk being left further behind. Closing the digital divide is part of the Sustainable Development Goals."]],
sp:["Climbing for a signal","During the COVID-19 pandemic, stories and photos spread of students in the Philippines and other countries climbing hills, trees and rooftops, or walking long distances to roadsides, to find a mobile signal strong enough to submit assignments or join online classes. In some communities, residents built small bamboo huts on hilltops as study spots with better reception. These images showed the determination of students, but also how unequal access to the internet could decide who was able to keep learning."],
g:[["Digital divide","The gap between those with and without digital access"],["Connectivity","The ability to connect to the internet"],["Digital literacy","The skills to use digital technology well"],["Accessibility","Design that allows people with disabilities to use something"]],
f:["More than 2 billion people are offline.","The Philippines passed a free public Wi-Fi law in 2017.","Many people first go online through mobile phones."],
fun:"During the pandemic, some students built bamboo huts on hilltops to get a better phone signal.",
tl:"Ang digital divide ay agwat sa pagitan ng may access sa internet at wala. Mahigit 2 bilyong tao pa rin ang walang internet. Sa Pilipinas, maraming malalayong lugar ang mahina ang signal."});

ADDART({id:"smarthome",t:"Smart Homes and the Internet of Things",c:"Technology",i:"🏠",w:"Internet of things",r:["internet","ai","dataprivacy","cybersecurity","elec"],
p:[["Connected things","The Internet of Things connects everyday objects to the internet."],["Smart homes","Lights, speakers, cameras and appliances can be controlled by phone or voice."],["Convenience and risks","Smart devices are convenient but raise privacy and security concerns."]],
l:"The Internet of Things, or IoT, is the growing network of everyday objects, such as lights, thermostats, door locks, cameras, appliances, watches, cars, factory machines and farm sensors, that are connected to the internet and can collect data, communicate and be controlled remotely. A smart home uses such devices so that people can control lighting, appliances, security and entertainment with a phone app or voice commands. IoT devices can save energy, improve safety and help older people and people with disabilities live independently, but they also raise concerns about privacy, hacking and electronic waste.",
s:[["Origins","In 1982 students at Carnegie Mellon University connected a soda machine to their network so they could check whether drinks were cold before walking to it. In 1999 the British technologist Kevin Ashton coined the phrase Internet of Things. Cheap sensors, wireless networks and smartphones later made connected devices common."],["Smart home devices","Common smart home devices include voice-controlled speakers, smart lights and plugs, security cameras and video doorbells, smart locks, thermostats and air conditioners, robot vacuum cleaners and smart televisions. Many can be programmed to follow schedules or respond to sensors."],["Voice assistants","Smart speakers with voice assistants, such as Amazon’s Alexa, launched in 2014, and Google Assistant, let people play music, set timers, check the weather and control devices by speaking. They use artificial intelligence to understand speech."],["Beyond the home","The IoT is used in cities to manage traffic lights and streetlights, in farms to monitor soil moisture and livestock, in hospitals to track equipment and patients, in factories to predict machine failures and in disaster monitoring, such as flood and weather sensors."]],
k:[["Term coined","1999, by Kevin Ashton"],["Early connected device","A soda machine at Carnegie Mellon (1982)"],["Smart speaker with Alexa launched","2014"],["Common standard","Matter (2022)"],["Main concerns","Privacy, security, e-waste"]],
d:[["1982","Students connect a soda machine to a network"],["1991","A coffee pot webcam goes online at Cambridge"],["1999","The term Internet of Things is coined"],["2014","Amazon launches a smart speaker with Alexa"],["2016","A huge cyberattack uses hacked IoT devices"],["2022","The Matter standard aims to make smart devices work together"]],
s2:[["Energy saving","Smart thermostats, air conditioners, lights and plugs can turn devices off when they are not needed and schedule them for cheaper times. Smart electricity meters help households and utilities track power use."],["Helping people live independently","Smart devices can help older people and people with disabilities by allowing voice control of lights and doors, sending reminders to take medicine and alerting family members or caregivers if someone falls."],["Security risks","Many IoT devices have weak security, such as default passwords that are never changed. In 2016 hackers used a program called Mirai to take over hundreds of thousands of poorly secured cameras and routers, using them to launch a huge attack that disrupted major websites. Experts advise changing default passwords and updating devices."],["Privacy","Smart speakers, cameras and other devices collect data about people’s homes and habits. Users should check privacy settings, know when devices are listening or recording and be careful about cameras in private spaces."],["Smart devices in the Philippines","Smart home devices, such as Wi-Fi plugs, cameras and smart TVs, are increasingly common in Filipino homes, along with community uses such as CCTV networks in barangays and sensors that warn of rising river levels during typhoons."],["Electronic waste","As smart devices multiply, they add to electronic waste, especially when they stop receiving software updates or their companies shut down services. Repairable designs and longer support periods can help."]],
sp:["The coffee pot that made history","In 1991, researchers at the University of Cambridge in England grew tired of walking to the coffee pot in the Trojan Room only to find it empty. They pointed a camera at the pot and wrote software so they could see a small picture of it on their computer screens. In 1993 the image was put on the World Wide Web, making it one of the first webcams. People around the world logged on just to watch a coffee pot. When it was finally switched off in 2001, the pot was sold at auction for thousands of pounds."],
g:[["Internet of Things","Everyday objects connected to the internet"],["Smart home","A home with devices controlled remotely or automatically"],["Sensor","A device that detects changes such as heat, light or motion"],["Voice assistant","Software that responds to spoken commands"]],
f:["The term Internet of Things was coined in 1999.","A Cambridge coffee pot was one of the first webcams.","Weak passwords make smart devices vulnerable to hackers."],
fun:"One of the first webcams showed a university coffee pot.",
tl:"Ang Internet of Things ay ang pagkonekta ng mga pang-araw-araw na bagay sa internet, gaya ng ilaw, kamera at appliances. Sa smart home, nakokontrol ang mga ito gamit ang cellphone o boses. May mga alalahanin sa privacy at seguridad."});

ADDART({id:"housing",t:"Housing and Homes",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🏘️",w:"Affordable housing",r:["urban","megacities","inequality","costliving","architecture"],
p:[["A place to live","Everyone needs a safe, decent and affordable home."],["A global challenge","Billions of people live in inadequate or unaffordable housing."],["Different solutions","Countries try public housing, new building, subsidies and slum upgrading."]],
l:"Housing is a basic human need and is recognized as a human right in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. Yet around the world, billions of people live in housing that is crowded, unsafe, insecure or too expensive, and around a billion live in slums or informal settlements. In many cities, house prices and rents have risen much faster than incomes, making homes unaffordable for young people and low-income families. Governments, builders, communities and charities try different solutions, from public housing and subsidies to slum upgrading and community-built homes, and the best approach is widely debated.",
s:[["What makes housing adequate","According to the United Nations, adequate housing means more than four walls and a roof: it includes security from eviction, access to water, sanitation and electricity, affordability, safety, enough space, access to jobs and schools and respect for culture."],["Why housing is expensive","House prices and rents rise when demand grows faster than supply, as when cities attract many workers. Land costs, building rules, slow approvals, interest rates, speculation and investors buying homes can all push prices up. Economists debate which factors matter most in each place."],["Informal settlements","In many fast-growing cities, people who cannot afford formal housing build homes on unused land, along rivers, railways or hillsides, often without secure ownership or services. These communities are vulnerable to floods, fires and evictions, but they also show great resourcefulness."],["Homelessness","Homelessness affects people in rich and poor countries alike. Causes include poverty, high rents, unemployment, family breakdown, disasters and mental illness. Approaches such as Housing First, which gives homeless people a stable home before addressing other problems, have shown success."]],
k:[["Human right","Recognized in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights"],["People in slums","Around 1 billion"],["Singapore public housing","About 80% of residents"],["Philippine housing program","Pambansang Pabahay para sa Pilipino (2023)"],["World Habitat Day","First Monday of October"]],
d:[["1948","The Universal Declaration of Human Rights recognizes the right to housing"],["1960","Singapore creates its Housing and Development Board"],["1976","Habitat for Humanity is founded"],["2003","Gawad Kalinga begins building communities in the Philippines"],["2016","The UN adopts the New Urban Agenda"],["2023","The Philippines launches a national housing program"]],
s2:[["Housing in the Philippines","The Philippines has a large housing backlog, estimated in the millions of units, and many families live in informal settlements, especially in Metro Manila. Government agencies, the Pag-IBIG Fund and private developers build housing, and the Pambansang Pabahay para sa Pilipino program, launched in 2023, aims to build large numbers of affordable homes. Critics have raised concerns about affordability and location."],["Singapore’s public housing","Since 1960, Singapore’s Housing and Development Board has built high-rise public housing estates where about 80 percent of residents live, most of them owning their flats on long leases. The system is admired for providing affordable, well-planned homes, though it relies on unique conditions such as state ownership of most land."],["Vienna’s social housing","In Vienna, Austria, a large share of residents live in social or city-owned housing, built since the 1920s, with good design and affordable rents, mixing people of different incomes. Many cities study Vienna’s model."],["Community-built homes","Organizations such as Habitat for Humanity, founded in 1976, and Gawad Kalinga in the Philippines, founded in 2003, help families build homes with volunteers, often requiring families to contribute their own labor, known as sweat equity, reflecting the Filipino tradition of bayanihan."],["Debates","Some argue that the main solution is to build many more homes by loosening building rules. Others emphasize rent controls, public and social housing, taxes on vacant homes and limits on speculation. Many experts suggest combining several approaches suited to local conditions."],["Building better","Homes in disaster-prone places, like the Philippines, need to withstand typhoons, floods and earthquakes. Architects are developing affordable, climate-resilient and energy-efficient designs, including homes built with bamboo and other local materials."]],
sp:["Bayanihan: moving a house together","In the traditional Filipino custom of bayanihan, when a family in a village needed to move, neighbors would gather to lift their entire nipa hut on bamboo poles and carry it on their shoulders to its new location, sometimes dozens of men walking together. In return, the family would prepare a meal for everyone. The image of villagers carrying a house became a symbol of community spirit in the Philippines, celebrated in art and the famous painting by Carlos Botong Francisco. Today the word bayanihan is used for any act of communities working together, including building homes for those in need."],
g:[["Adequate housing","Safe, secure, affordable housing with basic services"],["Informal settlement","An area of unplanned housing without secure ownership"],["Public housing","Homes built or owned by the government"],["Bayanihan","The Filipino spirit of community cooperation"]],
f:["Housing is recognized as a human right.","About 80 percent of Singapore residents live in public housing.","Bayanihan once meant carrying a neighbor’s house together."],
fun:"In traditional bayanihan, neighbors carried a whole nipa hut on their shoulders.",
tl:"Ang pabahay ay pangunahing pangangailangan at karapatang pantao. Bilyun-bilyong tao ang nakatira sa hindi sapat o mahal na tirahan. Sa Pilipinas, malaki ang kakulangan sa pabahay, at simbolo ng pagtutulungan ang bayanihan."});

ADDART({id:"loneliness",t:"Loneliness and Social Connection",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🤝",w:"Loneliness",r:["mental","family","agingworld","mentalyouth","socialmedia"],
p:[["Feeling alone","Loneliness is the painful feeling of having fewer close connections than you want."],["A public health issue","Health organizations now treat loneliness as a serious health concern."],["Connection helps","Friendships, family and community protect health and happiness."]],
l:"Loneliness is the distressing feeling that one’s social relationships are fewer or less close than one would like. It is different from being alone; people can feel lonely in a crowd or content in solitude. Research shows that long-lasting loneliness and social isolation are linked to higher risks of heart disease, stroke, depression, dementia and early death. In 2025 a World Health Organization commission estimated that about one in six people worldwide is affected by loneliness. Governments, health organizations and communities increasingly treat social connection as a public health priority.",
s:[["Loneliness and isolation","Social isolation means having few social contacts, while loneliness is the feeling of lacking connection. Both can harm health. Older people living alone, young people, people with disabilities, migrants and those who are poor or face discrimination may be especially at risk."],["Effects on health","Studies link chronic loneliness to higher blood pressure, weaker immunity, poorer sleep and greater risk of heart disease, depression and anxiety. Some researchers have compared the health risks of severe social disconnection with those of smoking."],["Causes","Loneliness can be caused by moving away, losing a loved one, illness, unemployment, long working hours, living alone, bullying or difficulties making friends. Changes in society, such as smaller families, urban living and less participation in community groups, may also contribute."],["Young people","Surprisingly, surveys in many countries find that young adults report loneliness as often as or more often than older people. The roles of social media and screens are debated: they can help people stay connected but may also replace face-to-face time."]],
k:[["People affected by loneliness","About 1 in 6 worldwide (WHO, 2025)"],["UK minister for loneliness","Appointed 2018"],["Japan minister for loneliness","Appointed 2021"],["WHO commission on social connection","Launched 2023"],["Best remedy","Meaningful relationships and community"]],
d:[["1938","A long-running Harvard study of adult life begins"],["2018","The United Kingdom appoints a minister for loneliness"],["2021","Japan appoints a minister for loneliness"],["2023","The WHO launches a commission on social connection"],["2023","US health officials call loneliness an epidemic"],["2025","A WHO report estimates loneliness affects one in six people"]],
s2:[["What helps","Ways to build connection include reaching out to friends and family regularly, joining clubs, sports teams, religious groups or volunteer activities, talking to neighbors, sharing meals and helping others. Even small interactions, such as greeting a shop owner, can lift mood."],["Communities in the Philippines","Filipino culture values close family ties, barkada friendships, fiestas and bayanihan, which can protect against loneliness. But many Filipinos experience long separations from loved ones, such as families of overseas workers. Video calls, community groups and shared celebrations help families stay close across distances."],["The Harvard study","Since 1938, a Harvard University study has followed hundreds of men, and later their families, throughout their lives. One of its main findings is that close, warm relationships are a strong predictor of long, healthy and happy lives, more than wealth or fame."],["Governments act","In 2018 the United Kingdom appointed a minister for loneliness, and Japan did the same in 2021. Some doctors now practice social prescribing, referring lonely patients to community activities such as gardening clubs, art classes or walking groups."],["Older people","Older people may become isolated after retirement, the death of a partner or loss of mobility. Programs that bring generations together, such as students visiting elderly neighbors or shared community centers, benefit both young and old."],["Being alone is not always lonely","Time alone can be restful and creative, and some people enjoy solitude. Loneliness becomes a problem when people feel unwanted, misunderstood or disconnected for long periods. Anyone who feels persistently lonely or low can talk to someone they trust or seek help from a health professional."]],
sp:["Chatty benches","In 2019, police in the town of Burnham-on-Sea in England put up signs on park benches reading: Happy to chat bench. Sit here if you don’t mind someone stopping to say hello. The idea was simple: anyone who sat on the bench was signaling that they would welcome a conversation with a stranger. Chat benches spread to many towns in the United Kingdom and other countries, from Australia to Canada, helping lonely people, especially older people, find someone to talk to. Some cafés followed with chatter tables where customers could join others for conversation."],
g:[["Loneliness","The painful feeling of lacking close connections"],["Social isolation","Having few social contacts"],["Social prescribing","Doctors referring patients to community activities"],["Barkada","A Filipino word for a close group of friends"]],
f:["About one in six people worldwide is affected by loneliness.","The UK appointed a minister for loneliness in 2018.","Close relationships predict long, healthy lives."],
fun:"Some towns have chat benches where sitting down means you are happy to talk.",
tl:"Ang kalungkutan o loneliness ay ang pakiramdam na kulang ang malapit na ugnayan. Maaari itong makasama sa kalusugan. Nakatutulong ang pamilya, barkada, komunidad at pakikipag-usap sa iba."});

ADDART({id:"urbanfarm",t:"Urban Farming",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🥬",w:"Urban agriculture",r:["agri","foodsecurity","urban","megacities","recycling"],
p:[["Farms in the city","Urban farming means growing food in and around cities."],["Rooftops and walls","Food grows on rooftops, balconies, vacant lots and even indoors."],["Fresh and local","City farms provide fresh food, green space and community."]],
l:"Urban farming, or urban agriculture, is the growing of food, and sometimes raising of animals, in and around cities, on rooftops, balconies, backyards, vacant lots, schoolyards, walls and even indoors. It ranges from family vegetable gardens and community gardens to high-tech vertical farms. Urban farming can provide fresh, nutritious food, income, green spaces, cooler neighborhoods and stronger communities, and it can make cities more resilient to food price shocks and disasters. In the Philippines, urban gardening grew rapidly during the COVID-19 pandemic. Challenges include limited space, soil contamination and costs.",
s:[["An old idea","People have always grown food near their homes. The Aztec capital Tenochtitlan fed its people with chinampas, floating gardens. During the World Wars, millions of people in Britain, the United States and elsewhere planted victory gardens in backyards and parks to grow food."],["Kinds of urban farms","Urban farms include home gardens, community gardens shared by neighbors, school gardens, rooftop farms, container gardens on balconies and indoor farms. Some raise chickens, fish, bees or mushrooms."],["Growing without soil","Hydroponics grows plants in nutrient-rich water instead of soil, and aquaponics combines fish tanks with plant beds, with fish waste fertilizing the plants. Vertical farms grow crops in stacked layers indoors under LED lights, using little water and space."],["Benefits","Urban farms provide fresh vegetables close to where people live, reduce transport, reuse food waste as compost, cool the air, absorb rainwater and create green spaces where neighbors meet. Gardening is also good exercise and can reduce stress."]],
k:[["Also called","Urban agriculture"],["Where food grows","Rooftops, balconies, vacant lots, indoors"],["Soil-free methods","Hydroponics, aquaponics"],["Historic example","Victory gardens in the World Wars"],["Philippine program","Plant, Plant, Plant (2020)"]],
d:[["1300s","The Aztecs farm chinampas in Tenochtitlan"],["1940s","Victory gardens spread during World War II"],["1990s","Havana turns to urban organic farms during an economic crisis"],["2010","Quezon City launches an urban farming program"],["2019","Singapore sets a goal to grow more of its own food"],["2020","The Philippines promotes home gardening during the pandemic"]],
s2:[["Havana’s organopónicos","When the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991, Cuba lost much of its imported food, fuel and fertilizer, causing severe shortages. In response, people in Havana turned vacant lots and rooftops into organic gardens called organopónicos, which produced large amounts of vegetables for the city. Havana became a famous example of urban farming."],["Urban gardening in the Philippines","During the COVID-19 pandemic, many Filipinos started growing vegetables at home in pots, recycled containers and small plots. The Department of Agriculture’s Plant, Plant, Plant program distributed seeds and seedlings, and local governments, such as Quezon City with its Joy of Urban Farming program, supported community gardens. Popular crops include pechay, kangkong, tomatoes, eggplant and okra."],["Singapore","Singapore, a small island city-state that imports most of its food, set a goal in 2019 to produce much more of its own food, encouraging rooftop farms, vertical farms and fish farms. Progress proved harder than expected, and the targets were later revised."],["Challenges","City soil may be contaminated by lead or other pollutants, so gardeners often test soil or use raised beds and containers. Space, water, sunlight, time and the cost of high-tech systems can also be challenges. Urban farms can supplement but not replace rural farming."],["Schools and gardens","School gardens teach children where food comes from, how plants grow and how to eat healthily. In the Philippines, the Gulayan sa Paaralan program encourages schools to grow vegetables, which can be used in school feeding programs."],["Start your own","Anyone can start small: herbs such as basil or tanglad in a pot, kangkong in a bucket of water, or vegetables in recycled plastic bottles. Compost from kitchen scraps can enrich the soil, turning waste into food."]],
sp:["Kangkong in a bottle","One of the easiest vegetables to grow in a small space is kangkong, water spinach, a favorite in Filipino dishes such as adobong kangkong and sinigang. Gardeners can take kangkong stems bought at the market, remove the leaves for cooking and place the stems in a container of water or moist soil near a sunny window. Within days, roots appear and new leaves sprout, ready to harvest within weeks. Many Filipino families grow kangkong in cut plastic bottles hung on walls or fences, turning trash into a small garden."],
g:[["Urban agriculture","Growing food in and around cities"],["Hydroponics","Growing plants in water with nutrients instead of soil"],["Vertical farm","An indoor farm with crops grown in stacked layers"],["Compost","Decayed organic matter used to enrich soil"]],
f:["Victory gardens fed people during the World Wars.","Hydroponics grows plants without soil.","Many Filipinos started home gardens during the pandemic."],
fun:"Kangkong can regrow from market stems placed in a bottle of water.",
tl:"Ang urban farming ay pagtatanim ng pagkain sa lungsod, gaya ng sa bubong, balkonahe at bakanteng lote. Lumaganap ito sa Pilipinas noong pandemya. Madaling magtanim ng kangkong, pechay at kamatis sa maliit na espasyo."});
